Done. Here's what was added to `swift/Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift` (lines 10–27):

```swift
public enum NoteParseError: Error, Equatable {
    case malformedFormat
}

public func parseNote(from text: String) throws -> Note {
    let parts = text.split(separator: "\n---\n")
    guard parts.count == 4 else {
        throw NoteParseError.malformedFormat
    }

    return Note(
        id: String(parts[0]),
        title: String(parts[1]),
        pinned: String(parts[2]) == "1",
        body: String(parts[3]),
        tags: extractTags(from: String(parts[3]))
    )
}
```

Key design points:

- **Split on `"\n---\n"`** — the same literal separator the Rust CLI uses. If the split doesn't produce exactly 4 parts, `NoteParseError.malformedFormat` is thrown.
- **`pinned`** is parsed by checking `parts[2] == "1"` (false for anything else, including "0" or missing).
- **`tags`** are extracted from the body via the existing `extractTags(from:)` — no duplication.
- **No defaulting or guessing** — malformed input fails fast with a clear error type.


